/pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC444279/)\nciteturn27view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC444279/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\nL0: Checking your browser before accessing pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ...\nL1: \nL2: Click 【0†here】 if you are not automatically redirected after 5 seconds.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Leishmania spp: completely defined medium without serum and macromolecules | Academia (https://www.academia.edu/74282665/Leishmania_spp_completely_defined_medium_without_serum_and_macromolecules_CDM_LP_for_the_continuous_in_vitro_cultivation_of_infective_promastigote_forms)\nciteturn27view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.academia.edu/74282665/Leishmania_spp_completely_defined_medium_without_serum_and_macromolecules_CDM_LP_for_the_continuous_in_vitro_cultivation_of_infective_promastigote_forms\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 96\nL0: # Leishmania spp: completely defined medium without serum and macromolecules (CDM/LP) for the continuous in vitro cultivation of infective promastigote forms\nL1: Am. J. Trop. Med. Hyg., 60(1), 1999, pp.\nL2: 41–50 Copyright q 1999 by The American Society of Tropical Medicine and Hygiene LEISHMANIA SPP.: COMPLETELY DEFINED MEDIUM WITHOUT SERUM AND MACROMOLECULES (CDM/LP) FOR THE CONTINUOUS IN VITRO CULTIVATION OF INFECTIVE PROMASTIGOTE FORMS TIMOTHEE MERLEN, DENIS SERENO, NATHALIE BRAJON, FLORENCE ROSTAND, AND JEAN-LOUP LEMESRE Institut Français de Recherche Scientifique pour le Développement en Coopération (ORSTOM), Laboratoire de Biologie Parasitaire, Montpellier, France Abstract.\nL3: The elimination of serum or of serum-derived macromolecules that supplant the fetal calf serum re- quirement from Leishmania culture media could decrease costs and improve the feasibility of large-scale production of well-defined parasite material.\nL4: We report a completely defined medium, without serum-derived protein and/or macromolecules as a serum substitute, of common, available, and inexpensive constituents that can be used in place of serum-supplemented media for the continuous in vitro cultivation of promastigote forms of various Leishmania species. Typical promastigote morphology was observed in Giemsa-stained smears, regardless of the strain analyzed.\nL5: Electrophoretic analysis showed that the proteinase patterns of aserically grown promastigote forms were similar to those obtained in serum-supplemented RPMI 1640 medium for all Leishmania studied. Similar antigenic profiles were recognized in immunoblots by sera from hosts with visceral or cutaneous leishmaniasis after growing promastigotes in the two different culture media.\nL6: For parasites causing both cutaneous and visceral leishmaniasis, the absence of serum and macromolecules in the culture medium did not markedly change their in vitro infectivity for resident mouse macrophages and their virulence in animals compared with parasites cultivated in nondefined medium. Serum-free technology will be increasingly important in providing stability and reproducibility as research using promastigote moves closer to therapeutic applications.\nL7: Parasites from the genus Leishmania cause a variety of hemolyzed rabbit blood,14 and semi-defined HOMEN’s me- disease states in humans and other mammals in tropical and dium15 have been reported. Serum-enriched tissue culture subtropical areas, which include cutaneous, mucocutaneous, media, initially formulated for the cultivation of mammalian and visceral leishmaniasis.\nL8: The parasite undergoes a digenic or insect cells, have been successfully used to cultivate sev- life cycle between a nonmotile intracellular amastigote stage eral Old and New World Leishmania species.\nL9: These include parasitizing the mammalian phagocytic cells and a flagellat- 199H medium, RPMI 1640 medium, Dulbecco’s minimal es- ed, motile promastigote stage in the midgut of its sandfly sential medium,5,16 Schneider’s Drosophila medium, and vector.1 A similar promastigote form develops when para- Grace’s insect tissue culture medium.16–18 However, all of sites are cultured in cell-free medium.2 these tissue culture media require addition of high concen- In vitro standardized cultivation of the members of the trations of fetal bovine serum (10–30%) as an essential fac- Leishmania genus is a useful approach for yielding amount tor for long-term growth of Leishmania promastigotes.\nL10: of parasites suitable for diagnosis purposes to provide a bet- Defined media were developed for the cultivation of cer- ter knowledge of host-parasite relationships and for the de- tain Leishmania. Leishmania tarentolae has been cultured in termination of biologic and immunologic characteristics of a chemically defined medium;19 however, this medium could the parasite. One of the primary goals of culturists has been not support the growth of other Leishmania species.\nL11: At- to achieve the long-term maintenance of active and dividing tempts to replace serum by bovine serum albumin20,21 or mix- populations of different Leishmania species. The different ture of purine bases, vitamins, and bovine albumin fraction media developed over the past 90 years can be classified in IV22 were also made for cultivating L. donovani promasti- two major categories: semi-solid biphasic media and liquid gote forms. An easily prepared, nearly defined medium con- monophasic media.\nL12: Leishmania promastigotes were first taining salts, glucose, and tryptose (i.e., an LIT medium), to grown on diphasic blood agar (NNN),3,4 which was later en- which was added concentrated RPMI 1640 and 199 media, riched with bacteriologic additives such as brain heart in- was shown to be adequate for the cultivation of L. chagasi fusion,5 and is used today with various modifications of the liquid phase added to the solid one.6–9 These nondefined di- and L.\nL13: amazonensis promastigotes23 and other Leishmania phasic media are still used today for adaptation and culti- species.24 Media developed for serum-free growth of mam- vation of Leishmania strains directly isolated from both ver- malian cells were adapted for the serial cultivation of dif- tebrate and invertebrate hosts. However, they have some dis- ferent Leishmania species. These media were enriched with advantages.\nL14: For example, they are complex to prepare and large concentrations of certain amino acids, vitamins, bovine difficult to standardize. Moreover, they contained blood and albumin, hormones, peptide supplements,25–31 or more re- bacteriologic additives as important factors for parasite rep- cently, with human urine.32,33 lication, which complicate biologic and immunologic stud- In the present paper, we report a completely chemically ies.\nL15: defined culture medium free of serum, macromolecules, pro- Further progress has been made with the use of serum- teins, and peptides that readily supports the growth and enriched liquid monophasic media. Nondefined glucose-lac- maintenance of promastigote forms of various Leishmania talbumin-serum-hemoglobin medium, originally developed species without compromising parasite growth rates.\nL16: Mor- for African trypanosomes,10,11 liver infusion tryptose (LIT) phologic, biochemical, immunologic, and biologic properties medium,12 Panmede medium,13 modified NIH medium using of aserically grown promastigote forms are presented.\nL17: 41 42 MERLEN AND OTHERS TABLE 1 Composition of the completely defined medium (CDM/LP) that supports the continuous growth of Leishmania promastigotes Vitamins Nonessential amino acids L-ascorbic acid (C) 0.06 mM D1-Alanine 0.11 mM d-Biotin (H) 1.50 mM Asparagine 0.36 mM Folic acid 3.00 mM L-Aspartic acid 0.26 mM p-Aminobenzoic 9.00 mM L-Glutamic acid 0.34 mM Nicotinic acid 0.04 mM Glycine 0.28 mM Nicotinamide 9.40 mM L-Proline 0.20 mM D-Pantothenic acid 0.90 mM L-Serine 0.42 mM Pyridoxine·HCl (B6) 5.40 mM Glutathione 3.60 mM Pyridoxal·HCl 1.00 mM Hydroxyproline 0.17 mM Riboflavin (B2) 0.56 mM Thiamine·HCl (B1) 0.34 mM Salts, sugars, and nucleotides Cyanocobalamin (B12) 4.00 mM NaCl 139.00 mM Calciferol (D3) 0.05 mM KCl 7.00 mM Menadione·3 H2O (K3) 0.01 mM CaCl2 0.72 mM L-a-tocopherol (E) 0.01 mM MgSO4·7 H2O 0.61 mM Retinol (A) 0.07 mM Fe(NO3)3·9 H2O 0.36 mM Inositol 0.25 mM Na2HPO4 6.22 mM Choline 24.0 mM NaHCO3 24.00 mM Cholesterol 0.10 mM KH2PO4 0.09 mM Glucose 13.30 mM Essential amino acids Hemin 7.70 mM L-Arginine·HCl 1.90 mM HEPES 20.00 mM L-Cysteine·HCl 0.12 mM Sodium acetate 0.12 mM L-Cystine·H2O 0.60 mM D-ribose 0.66 mM L-Glutamine 2.30 mM 2-deoxyribose 0.74 mM L-Histidine·HCl·H2O 0.13 mM Tween 80 4 mg/l L-Isoleucine 0.48 mM ATP (Na2) 0.36 mM L-Leucine 0.60 mM Adenosine sulfate 8.60 mM L-Lysine·HCl 0.32 mM Guanine·HCl 0.32 mM L-Methionine 0.14 mM Hypoxanthine 0.52 mM L-Phenylalanine 0.16 mM Xanthine (Na) 0.50 mM L-Threonine 0.29 mM Uracil 0.62 mM L-Tryptophan 46.00 mM Thymine 0.48 mM L-Tyrosine 0.17 mM L-Valine 0.27 mM MATERIALS AND METHODS strains used in this work have been characterized by isoen- zyme analysis.34 All the cultured isolates were preserved in Parasites.\nL18: Promastigote forms of L. chagasi (MHOM/BR/ a cryobank research and reference collection. 79/LI-01), L. infantum (MHOM/MA/67/IT-263, clone 2 Culture methods. The qualitative and quantitative for- and clone 7), L. donovani (MHOM/IN/83/H570, MHOM/ mulation of the completely defined medium developed for IN(--)/61/L-13, MHOM/--/--/IT-2217, and MHOM/IN/80/ the continued cultivation of Leishmania promastigotes DD8, clone 2), L. mexicana (MNYC/BZ/62/M-379), L. (CDM/LP) is shown in Table 1.\nL19: This medium contains com- amazonensis (MHOM/VE/76/JAP78, MHOM/BR/76/LTB- mon salts, sugars, hemin, HEPES, purines, and purine nu- 012, MHOM/BR/73/M-2269, MHOM/BO/83/LPZ-155, and cleosides required by the parasites, a high concentration of MPRO/BR/